From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Peter Zahradnik <petzah@znik.co.uk>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 07:54:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: lantiq_gswip: enable assisted learning on CPU
 port

Frames injected by the CPU are not learned by the switch, so addresses
behind foreign bridge ports such as Wi-Fi interfaces are unknown to it.

When a station that was last seen behind a switch port (e.g. the uplink
to another access point) roams onto a local Wi-Fi interface, the switch
keeps its learned entry pointing at that port. Traffic for the station
arriving on the same port is then filtered in hardware instead of being
forwarded to the CPU, until the entry ages out after about 300 seconds.
DHCP OFFER and ACK packets are lost, and the client gives up and roams
away again.

Now that FDB entries on the CPU port are programmed into the hardware,
enable assisted learning on the CPU port so DSA installs the addresses
the bridge learns on foreign ports there. Writing the static CPU-port
entry for the same MAC address and FID replaces the stale learned entry.

This is the same approach other DSA drivers use for this problem:
mt7530, felix and sja1105 already enable assisted learning on the CPU
port, and commit 88c71cc0ad98 ("net: dsa: mxl862xx: enable assisted
learning on CPU port") fixed the same symptom (lost DHCP OFFER/ACK
after a client roams onto a local Wi-Fi interface) on MxL862xx.

Tested on a BT Home Hub 5A (xRX200, GSWIP 2.1) used as a wired access
point with a VLAN-aware bridge, with images built from OpenWrt 25.12.6
(kernel 6.12) and from main (kernel 6.18):
- without this patch, clients roaming onto the AP from another AP get
  no downlink traffic for about 20 seconds and then roam back
- with this patch, the stale uplink entry is gone as soon as the client
  associates, on both kernels and also right after a reboot
- in a 4 hour run per kernel with STP enabled, none of 25 roams onto
  the AP (8 on 6.12, 17 on 6.18) left a stale entry, and every DHCP
  request (about 1200 per run) was answered
- an earlier 6 hour test on 6.12 saw 14 working roams and 193 DHCP
  transactions without retransmissions

Link: https://github.com/openwrt/openwrt/issues/11650
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Peter Zahradnik <petzah@znik.co.uk>
---
 drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/lantiq_gswip.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/lantiq_gswip.c
+++ b/drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/lantiq_gswip.c
@@ -679,6 +679,15 @@ static int gswip_setup(struct dsa_switch
 
 	ds->configure_vlan_while_not_filtering = false;
 
+	/* Frames injected by the CPU are not learned by the switch, so
+	 * addresses behind foreign bridge ports (e.g. Wi-Fi) are unknown to
+	 * it. A station roaming from the uplink side onto such a port keeps
+	 * a stale learned entry pointing at the uplink and frames for it
+	 * coming in on that port are dropped until the entry ages out.
+	 * Let DSA install the bridge-learned addresses on the CPU port.
+	 */
+	ds->assisted_learning_on_cpu_port = true;
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
